{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-127427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052793/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b43e8de4a0065bb333a753d3c7cb9d0390d7d8305f4d18c659b51c073d29cad"} {"question" "q-127427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}+{cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14052793/state.json" "file_sha256" "0b43e8de4a0065bb333a753d3c7cb9d0390d7d8305f4d18c659b51c073d29cad"} {"question" "q-127427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/C